WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning, John, and merry Christmas!

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.540
So in the last few years, I couldn't help
but notice that in the lead-up to Christmas

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.400
there is this very strong desire among some
people to try and define exactly what Christmas

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.370
is, and then complain that it isn't being
done right.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.880
We're focusing too much on consumption, on
the worship of the acquisition of material

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.200
things, we're not considering the religious
importance of the holiday, we're not focusing

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.040
on the historical roots of the holiday, and
all of these traditions are co-opted from

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.260
cultures that we kind of helped destroy.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.910
Santa is a weird omniscient magical demi-god
that clearly anagrams to Satan, and the madness

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.320
of Christmas Eve consumerism is indicative
of everything wrong with Western culture.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.500
Here's what I think about all this.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.220
Culture is like light: it's all around us,
it makes our lives possible, it shows us where

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.950
to go, it defines how we see things, and it
helps us not stub our toes on weird institutions,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.180
like the fact that you shouldn't feed ramen
noodles to your parents when they come visit,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.740
and women can wear skirts but men have to
put each individual leg into a separate leg

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.210
prison, but you can't actually see light.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.800
You can only see what light hits.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.430
In the same way, even though culture informs
every bit of our lives, it's really difficult

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:01.430
to see.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:05.830
Holidays and rites of passage are lenses that
focus that cultural light into forms that

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.000
we can actually see and hold and understand
and critique, and since Christmas, or more

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.900
broadly like, the holiday giving season, is
our culture's biggest holiday.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.960
It is a time when our culture is very visible,
and so too are all of the bits of our culture

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.640
that don't quite jive with our worldview.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.020
But unlike a lens that just focuses the light,
holidays actually give us an opportunity to

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.369
change the nature of the light, change the
nature of culture.

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.530
It's an opportunity to look at what our society
is made of, and maybe say, I don't want that

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.530
bit.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.500
We can give charitable contributions instead
of gifts, we can focus on the happy wonder

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.900
of children or the Solstice or Jesus or whatever
tradition feels deepest and richest to you,

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.280
while doing our very best not to whine about
the fact that other people are doing it differently,

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.710
because the things that they value and the
culture that they live in is different from

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:54.000
ours, because they're different people.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.630
For me this time of year, it's friends, it's
video games, it's Christmas trees, it's Ella

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.780
Fitzgerald, it's Buddy the Elf's breathless
enthusiasm infecting the whole world, it's

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.340
cats on Christmas trees.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.160
This holiday season, whatever you want to
call it, has a really deep and diverse group

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.560
of traditions to draw on, because our culture
is made up of a really deep and diverse group

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.250
of people, and in the end, what shines through
all of these traditions is giving.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.060
Wanting to make the lives of other people
richer and better so that they know that we

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.630
care about them, and that's a bit of our culture
that I have a hard time having a problem with.

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.010
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

